  "changes": [
    "Certified bounded graph grouping inputs store primitive captured key/scalar values and undo by value. Original Dense/Pair validation precedes conversion; generic/custom/borrowed/unbound/standalone/explicit-batch paths retain their existing backend. Public group summary and match owners remain separately stored.",
    "Ordinary direct scoring, membership filters, maps and integer grouping retain concrete callback types through their private Node implementations. Original execution bodies, callback/Clone/Drop phases, live-commit certification and weighted metadata remain shared or unchanged; weighted and explicit-batched aliases keep boxed callbacks.",
    "Primitive flat-map graph caches omit an existing equal usize length only after all mapping, iterator and output identity work. Absent zero expansions, removals, standalone/custom paths and all downstream outputs are preserved.",
    "Eligible primitive terminals locate the old contribution once and commit through the original prepared insert/remove after arithmetic and payload cloning. Equal public row writes are not omitted; canonical keys, new value owners and public captures retain their original phases."
  ],
  "tradeoffs": [
    "Private bounded group snapshots now copy numeric values instead of sharing input-row Arcs. Sparse pair pages have larger cells; public snapshots and separate group/match owners retain their original behavior.",
    "Concrete callbacks increase per-closure code generation; this observation does not establish a build-time or code-size improvement. The general fat-LTO release profile, compiler, target CPU, panic policy, original model/fixtures/settings and benchmark runner are unchanged.",
    "The separately measured duplicate-state grouping shortcut was slower alone and is not integrated. Earlier combination and selected combination controls used separate sessions; do not compare their raw medians as an interleaved speedup.",
    "Scores and feasibility are separate; no equal-score or identical search-trace gate. Full default qualification above determines the two actual Java throughput targets."
  ],
